3	//! `AuthenticateApiKey` use case: validates a presented API-key token (cache-first hot path,
4	//! spec §9) and resolves it to its service account's `AuthnPrincipal` (SMA-445, M4, Task 18).
5	//! Mirrors `AuthenticateToken`'s structure (`application/authenticate_token.rs`) — same
6	//! `AuthnError` vocabulary, same `introspect` paging shape — but for the API-key credential
7	//! path instead of OIDC.
8	//!
9	//! **Cache-first hot path (spec §9/D5):** `resolve` parses the token (`parse_token`, core), then
10	//! checks `ApiKeyValidationCache::get(key_id)` BEFORE ever touching Postgres. A HIT ALWAYS
11	//! re-verifies the presented secret against the cached peppered `key_hash`
12	//! (`hasher.verify(secret, cached.key_hash)`, constant-time) and re-checks `expires_at` + the
13	//! cached `sa_status` fresh (all computed at read time, never trusted stale) — the two things a
14	//! hit saves are the DB round-trips (`find_by_id` + `find_principal`), NOT the secret check. The
15	//! `key_id` a hit is keyed by is a NON-secret token segment (`pgs_sk_<keyid>_<secret>`), so
16	//! authenticating on a cache hit WITHOUT re-verifying the secret would be an auth-bypass: any
17	//! secret paired with a cached `key_id` would pass for up to the cache TTL. Caching the stored
18	//! hash (the SAME peppered HMAC in Postgres, safe to cache — a Redis leak can't validate keys
19	//! without the pepper) is what lets the hit path stay a real accelerator AND still prove secret
20	//! possession. `cache` is `Arc<dyn ApiKeyValidationCache>` (not generic-DI) so it is the exact
21	//! same shared instance `ServiceAccountService::archive`/`ApiKeyService::revoke` evict through —
22	//! a revoke/archive's eviction is what bounds the (revocation-vs-cache) staleness window to one
23	//! TTL (`service_accounts.rs`/`cache.rs` module docs).
24	//!
25	//! **Unconditional SA-status check on every miss (D16, carry-forward from Task 17's review):**
26	//! `resolve_uncached` ALWAYS reads the service account's live `Principal` via
27	//! `PrincipalRepository::find_principal` and asserts `status == PrincipalStatus::Active` before
28	//! ever caching a positive validation or returning a principal — this is the SOLE thing that
29	//! stops a key issued for (or belonging to) a Disabled SA from authenticating (mirrors
30	//! `AuthenticateToken::resolve`'s own principal-status guard, `authenticate_token.rs:127`).
31	//!
32	//! **`ApiKey.scope_actions`/`scope_roles` are NEVER read here (spec D4):** they are stored,
33	//! v1-UNENFORCED metadata (`ApiKeyService::issue`'s own doc, `api_keys.rs`) — authorization comes
34	//! ONLY from the SA's M3 role grants, later, at authz time. This use case ONLY authenticates.
35	
36	use crate::adapters::api_keys::{ApiKeyValidationCache, CachedValidation};
37	use crate::config::ApiKeyConfig;
38	use paigasus_iam_core::{
39	    ApiKeyDefect, ApiKeyId, ApiKeyRepository, ApiKeyStatus, AuthnError, AuthnPrincipal, Clock, Credential, MembershipRepository, ParsedToken, PrincipalContext, PrincipalKind, PrincipalRepository,
40	    PrincipalStatus, RepositoryError, SecretHasher, TokenDefect, parse_token,
41	};
42	use std::sync::Arc;
43	
44	/// `list_by_principal` page size for introspection's membership assembly — identical to
45	/// `AuthenticateToken`'s own constant (`authenticate_token.rs:49`), same rationale (§6.1).
46	const MEMBERSHIP_PAGE_SIZE: u64 = 200;
47	
48	/// Wraps any `RepositoryError` as `AuthnError::Backend` — the catch-all for repository failures
49	/// this use case doesn't specifically interpret, mirroring `authenticate_token.rs::backend`.
50	fn backend(err: RepositoryError) -> AuthnError {
51	    AuthnError::Backend(Box::new(err))
52	}
53	
54	/// Maps this file's own [`ApiKeyDefect`] — `parse_token`'s parse failures, plus the
55	/// revoked/expired/bad-secret outcomes this use case determines from a DB (or cache) read — onto
56	/// `AuthnError::InvalidToken`'s shared `TokenDefect` vocabulary. `TokenDefect` predates API keys
57	/// (OIDC-only, `authn.rs`) and stays that way: `AuthnError` is deliberately ONE shape across both
58	/// credential kinds rather than growing a second, API-key-specific `InvalidToken` variant.
59	/// `Display` never distinguishes ANY of these (both `ApiKeyDefect` and `AuthnError::InvalidToken`
60	/// scrub to a single generic message, `api_key.rs`/`authn.rs`) — this mapping only affects
61	/// `Debug`, i.e. tests/internal logs, so the specific `TokenDefect` chosen per variant is a
62	/// best-effort nearest-analog, not a load-bearing distinction:
63	/// - [`ApiKeyDefect::Malformed`] (parse failure, or an unrecognized `key_id`) -> `Malformed`.
64	/// - [`ApiKeyDefect::BadSecret`] (HMAC verification failed) -> `BadSignature`.
65	/// - [`ApiKeyDefect::Revoked`]/[`ApiKeyDefect::Expired`] -> `Expired` — neither has a dedicated
66	///   `TokenDefect` analog; `Expired` is the nearest existing "was valid once, isn't now" concept
67	///   (as opposed to `Malformed`/`BadSignature`'s "never structurally valid to begin with").
68	fn invalid_token(defect: ApiKeyDefect) -> AuthnError {
69	    let kind = match defect {
70	        ApiKeyDefect::Malformed => TokenDefect::Malformed,
71	        ApiKeyDefect::BadSecret => TokenDefect::BadSignature,
72	        ApiKeyDefect::Revoked | ApiKeyDefect::Expired => TokenDefect::Expired,
73	    };
74	    AuthnError::InvalidToken(kind)
75	}
