3	//! API-key introspection cache (spec §9/D5): a positive-validation cache keyed by `keyid`,
4	//! sitting in front of the Postgres source of truth on `AuthenticateApiKey`'s hot path (parse
5	//! token → `cache.get(keyid)` → hit re-verifies the secret + re-checks expiry + cached
6	//! `sa_status`, miss falls through to a DB validate + `cache.put`). [`CachedValidation`] carries
7	//! only what's needed to rebuild an `AuthnPrincipal`, re-check expiry, and re-verify the
8	//! presented secret on read: the resolved principal id, the SA status, the key expiry, and the
9	//! stored peppered HMAC hash (`key_hash`) — **never** the plaintext secret. Keying by `keyid`
10	//! (a non-secret token segment) is what makes eviction on revoke/archive work; caching the
11	//! `key_hash` alongside is what stops that same non-secret key from authenticating WITHOUT the
12	//! secret — the caller MUST `SecretHasher::verify` the presented secret against `key_hash` on
13	//! every hit (that check is the credential; the `keyid` is only the lookup key).
14	//!
15	//! Two implementations, mirroring `adapters::authz::decision_cache` exactly: [`MemoryApiKeyCache`]
16	//! (single-replica, TTL-bounded `Mutex<HashMap<..>>`) and [`RedisApiKeyCache`] (cross-replica,
17	//! `ConnectionManager`, same connect/clone-per-call pattern as `RedisDecisionCache`/`SliceCache`).
18	//!
19	//! **Both fail OPEN (D5):** this cache is a pure accelerator over the Postgres-backed
20	//! `ApiKeyRepository` — never the system of record — so a `get` that can't be served cleanly (a
21	//! Redis error, or a payload that fails to deserialize) is reported as a plain cache miss,
22	//! `None`, never an error; a `put`/`evict` that can't be written is logged and swallowed. A
23	//! Redis outage bypasses the accelerator; it must never fail (or falsely succeed) a validation.
24	//!
25	//! **Revocation-vs-cache honesty (challenge M5, spec §9):** `MemoryApiKeyCache` evicts only the
26	//! local replica's entries — it is single-replica/dev only; multi-replica (HA) deployments must
27	//! use [`RedisApiKeyCache`] so `RevokeApiKey`/`ArchiveServiceAccount` evictions are global. Even
28	//! on Redis, a put-after-evict race (a concurrent `resolve` that DB-validated *before* a revoke
29	//! and `put`s *after* the evict) can re-seed a positive entry for up to one TTL — hence the
30	//! short default TTL (30s). This cache never re-checks `expires_at` itself on a hit; that's the
31	//! caller's job against the cached value (expiry has no staleness problem — it's recomputed
32	//! fresh from `CachedValidation.expires_at` on every read).
